On 2006-05-16, Jakob Creutzig wrote:
>
> I never thought that throwing heavier objects causes you to be pushed 
> further away.  Is this true?  If so, I call possible violation of 
> Newton laws; it's the impulse I give the object which determines my 
> own acceleration, not the weight of the object.  If I throw a wand as 
> far as I can (seems like a logical assumption to me that throwing wands 
> or such stuff is done "with all your might"), I would naively think that 
> this creates roughly the same impulse as when throwing, say, a chest 
> full of plate armor as far as I can.  On the other hand, it might be 
> true that the human body is capable of creating more impulse when 
> throwing heavy objects.  

I would think that human anatomy imposes a limit on the escape _speed_ of a
thrown object. If mass of object * limit speed is lower than the momentum your
muscles can manage, then a heavier object would result in more propulsion by
Newton. I'd also think that this would stop being an issue at the weight of
chests, at least.

Of course, to be realistic you'd also have to consider the shape of the
object, as that determines how it can be thrown (something with a handle can
be flung around before releas to achieve greater speed, then moment of inertia
becomes interesting)...

